\ A load that fails (file can't be opened) must NOT be recorded for reload.
\ Before the fix, reload re-ran the missing file and errored a second time.

"definitely-not-a-file.telic" load
reload
"survived" . cr

clear

\ Text save / load round-trip. `save` writes user vocabulary only
\ (definitions, no values, no stack). `load` re-runs the file as source.

: double 2 * ;
: square dup * ;
variable counter
42 to counter
symbol red
"/tmp/lf_save_test.telic" save

\ Throw away the user vocabulary, including counter's value.
forget double

\ Reload from the saved file — definitions come back, counter's slot
\ exists again but starts uninitialized (no value emitted by save).
"/tmp/lf_save_test.telic" load

\ Definitions work.
8 double .
3 square .
\ Symbol survives.
red .
\ Variable is reachable but its old value was not preserved.
counter .

clear

\ load ( s -- ) runs a source file as if typed: the file's top-level code
\ executes at load time (the set-map line prints during the load) and the words
\ it defines stay callable afterwards. The file is written here rather than kept
\ beside the test, so nothing extra sits in the runner's tests/*.telic glob.

": double 2 * ;
: square dup * ;
[< 1 2 3 >] ' square map .
" "/tmp/telic_008_loadable.telic" write-file

"/tmp/telic_008_loadable.telic" load
7 double .                                      \ 14
7 square .                                      \ 49

clear

\ A load-time error carries a file:line prefix: the loader knows the file, and
\ the line is the newline count to the failing token. Caught here with try-catch
\ so the located :message shows; an uncaught one prints the same at the REPL.

\ compile error on line 3
"3 4 + drop
5 6 * drop
bogus drop
" "/tmp/lf141a.telic" write-file
[: "/tmp/lf141a.telic" load :] [: :message @ . cr :] try-catch   \ ...lf141a.telic:3: unknown word: bogus

\ a runtime fault in top-level code is located at the triggering line
"1 2 + drop
3 0 /
" "/tmp/lf141b.telic" write-file
[: "/tmp/lf141b.telic" load :] [: :message @ . cr :] try-catch   \ ...lf141b.telic:2: division by zero

\ unterminated definition -> located at the end-of-file line
": f 1 2 +
" "/tmp/lf141c.telic" write-file
[: "/tmp/lf141c.telic" load :] [: :message @ . cr :] try-catch   \ ...lf141c.telic:2: unterminated definition

\ nested load: the error locates to the innermost file, not the outer one
"oops drop
" "/tmp/lf141inner.telic" write-file
"1 drop
""/tmp/lf141inner.telic"" load
" "/tmp/lf141outer.telic" write-file
[: "/tmp/lf141outer.telic" load :] [: :message @ . cr :] try-catch   \ ...lf141inner.telic:1: unknown word: oops

clear

\ ==== evaluate ====

\ evaluate ( str -- ): run a string's characters as source, as if typed there.

\ === a definition in the text enters the dictionary ===
": doubled 2 * ;" evaluate
21 doubled . cr                                      \ 42

\ === the text runs against the same stacks ===
"3 4 +" evaluate . cr                                \ 7

\ === newlines in the text are ordinary source ===
": tripled 3 * ;{nl}5 tripled . cr" format evaluate  \ 15

\ === evaluated text may itself evaluate ===
"""9 . cr"" evaluate" evaluate                       \ 9

\ === errors ===
[: "nosuchword" evaluate :] catch drop :message @ . cr    \ (no file prefix, unlike load)
[: ": broken 1" evaluate :] catch drop :message @ . cr    \ (unterminated definition)
[: "1 0 /" evaluate :] catch drop :message @ . cr         \ (the failure inside the text)
[: 42 evaluate :] catch drop :message @ . cr              \ (not a string)
"still here" . cr

clear
